<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Griqualand West</h1><p class="st-meta">1813 CE – 1871 CE (approx.) · capital Griquatown</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Griqua community of Griquatown under the Waterboer family, whose land claims became the legal key to the Kimberley diamond fields.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griqua">Griqua</a>, people of mixed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khoekhoe">Khoekhoe</a> and settler descent from the Cape, had gathered at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-missionary-society">London Missionary Society</a> station of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griquatown">Klaarwater</a> north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river">Orange River</a>. In 1813 the missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-campbell-missionary">John Campbell</a> helped them adopt a written constitution and renamed the place Griquatown, under the leaders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-kok-ii">Adam Kok II</a> and Barend Barends.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The community was governed by elected kapteins and a council, with mission influence strong in law, schooling and church life. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-kok-ii">Adam Kok II</a> moved away, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andries-waterboer">Andries Waterboer</a>, kaptein from 1820, made a treaty with the Cape in 1834 that brought a small subsidy and guns. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griqua">Griqua</a> lived by herding, hunting and trading, and spoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-dutch">Cape Dutch</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When diamonds were found in the late 1860s, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaas-waterboer">Nicolaas Waterboer's</a> claim to the diggings was upheld by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keate-award">Keate Award</a>, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> annexed the territory in 1871, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/griqualand-west-colony">Griqualand West Colony</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/griqualand-west.jpg" alt="Griqualand West" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Seb az86556 · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FlagGriekwalandEast.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1813 CE – 1871 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Griquatown</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Adam Kok II, Andries Waterboer, Nicolaas Waterboer</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Cape Dutch, Khoekhoe</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Griqua state of Griquatown, Waterboer's Griqua</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1842&amp;focus=griqualand-west" aria-label="Griqualand West on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5327 3229 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Griqualand West lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5647 3485L4997 651M5647 3485L5647 3485"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M5647 3485h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5647 3485h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5647 3485h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1842&amp;focus=griqualand-west">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
